<Layouts.public flash={@flash} current_user={@current_user} return_to={@return_to}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl space-y-8">
    <.header>
      Sign in with Pocket ID
      <:subtitle>One passkey identity for your app suite, wired app by app.</:subtitle>
    </.header>

    <%!-- #511 v1: the wiring guide, docs and catalog data only. The
    callback paths mirror the catalog's oidc_callback_path values (the
    seed tests pin both ends). Plain sentences by design: no em-dashes,
    no semicolons. --%>
    <.card class="space-y-4" data-testid="sign-in-with-pocket-id-guide">
      <h2 class="text-base font-medium text-ink">What this gives you</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Pocket ID is the identity app in the catalog. Deploy it, claim
        it, add a passkey, and your other apps can hand their sign-in
        to it.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Six catalog apps consume it today: Forgejo, Kavita,
        Vaultwarden, ActualBudget, Memos, and Vikunja. Five of them
        speak OpenID Connect natively. Memos connects through its
        Custom SSO template with the endpoints entered by hand.
      </p>
    </.card>

    <.card class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-base font-medium text-ink">Deploy Pocket ID first</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
        The order matters for three of the six. Vaultwarden,
        ActualBudget, and Vikunja read their provider settings from
        environment inputs that the deploy form collects, so their
        Pocket ID clients must exist before those apps deploy. An app
        that is already running is wired by
        redeploying it with the values. Forgejo, Kavita, and Memos are
        configured inside the app after deploy, so their order is
        loose.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Keep the Pocket ID instance public. An app's server fetches
        tokens from Pocket ID directly, so the issuer URL has to work
        from the app's container and not only in your browser.
      </p>
    </.card>

    <.card class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-base font-medium text-ink">Keep Pocket ID awake</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Run the identity instance always-on. Pick its size on the
        deploy form as usual, then turn on Always-on on the instance
        page after deploy. Always-on is not a plan tier. The instance
        simply never sleeps, and it never stops accruing its running
        rate.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Here is what sleeping does instead. A browser that opens the
        URL sees a waking page while the instance comes up. Your apps'
        servers get an error with a retry hint, so the first sign-in
        after a wake can fail. Already-signed-in sessions keep working.
        New sign-ins fail while the identity sleeps, and every app that
        points at it fails with them.
      </p>
    </.card>

    <.card class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-base font-medium text-ink">Create one client per app</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
        In Pocket ID open Administration, then OIDC Clients, then Add
        OIDC Client. Name the client after the app and add its callback
        URL. The callback URL is your app's instance URL plus the path
        from this table.
      </p>
      <div class="space-y-2 text-sm">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-4 rounded-lg bg-panel-raised px-3 py-2">
          <span class="font-medium text-ink">Forgejo</span>
          <span class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-muted">/user/oauth2/PocketID/callback</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-4 rounded-lg bg-panel-raised px-3 py-2">
          <span class="font-medium text-ink">Kavita</span>
          <span class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-muted">/signin-oidc</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-4 rounded-lg bg-panel-raised px-3 py-2">
          <span class="font-medium text-ink">Vaultwarden</span>
          <span class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-muted">/identity/connect/oidc-signin</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-4 rounded-lg bg-panel-raised px-3 py-2">
          <span class="font-medium text-ink">ActualBudget</span>
          <span class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-muted">/openid/callback</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-4 rounded-lg bg-panel-raised px-3 py-2">
          <span class="font-medium text-ink">Memos</span>
          <span class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-muted">/auth/callback</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-4 rounded-lg bg-panel-raised px-3 py-2">
          <span class="font-medium text-ink">Vikunja</span>
          <span class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-muted">/auth/openid/pocketid</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Choose who may sign in under Allowed User Groups, or pick All
        Users. The client's page shows the client ID, the client
        secret, and the discovery URL. The secret is shown once, so
        copy it when you create the client. For the Vaultwarden
        client, turn on PKCE. For the Vikunja client, leave PKCE off.
      </p>
    </.card>

    <.card class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-base font-medium text-ink">Wire Forgejo</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
        In Forgejo open Site Administration, then Identity &amp; Access,
        then Authentication Sources. Add a source of type OAuth2 with
        the OpenID Connect provider. Name it PocketID, because the name
        is part of the callback path. Paste the discovery URL, the
        client ID, and the client secret from Pocket ID. Set the
        additional scopes to openid email profile.
      </p>
    </.card>

    <.card class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-base font-medium text-ink">Wire Kavita</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
        In Kavita open Settings, then Server, then OpenID Connect.
        Enter the Pocket ID URL as the Authority with no trailing
        slash, plus the client ID and the client secret. Save and
        restart Kavita for the settings to take effect. In Pocket ID
        you can also add /signout-callback-oidc as the client's logout
        callback. If you sync roles from a claim, the new roles apply
        after a full sign-out and sign-in.
      </p>
    </.card>

    <.card class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-base font-medium text-ink">Wire Vaultwarden</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Set the four inputs on the deploy form: enable Pocket ID
        sign-in, the issuer URL, the client ID, and the client secret.
        The issuer URL is your Pocket ID instance's public URL, exactly.
        Vaultwarden uses PKCE by default, which matches the client you
        created with PKCE on.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Two honest notes. The master password still unlocks the vault.
        SSO only gates the front door. And keep a second passkey for
        Pocket ID somewhere outside Vaultwarden, because a passkey that
        lives only inside the vault locks the identity behind the vault.
      </p>
    </.card>

    <.card class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-base font-medium text-ink">Wire ActualBudget</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Set the discovery URL, the client ID, and the client secret on
        the deploy form. The discovery URL is your Pocket ID instance's
        public URL. The instance's own URL is filled in for you. The
        first successful OpenID sign-in becomes the server owner, so
        make it yours.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Household members get accounts on their first sign-in if you
        set the creation mode input to login. Otherwise pre-create them
        in Actual's Users screen with matching usernames.
      </p>
    </.card>

    <.card class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-base font-medium text-ink">Wire Memos</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
        In Memos open Settings, then SSO, then Create. Pick the Custom
        template. Memos reads no discovery URL, so paste the
        authorization, token, and userinfo endpoints from the client's
        page in Pocket ID. Show more details lists them. Set the
        identifier to preferred_username, the display name to name, and
        the email field to email. Set the scopes to openid email
        profile.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Keep one admin password login as your recovery path, even after
        you disable passwords for regular users. Memos never links an
        SSO identity to an existing account by username.
      </p>
    </.card>

    <.card class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-base font-medium text-ink">Wire Vikunja</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Set the five Pocket ID inputs on the deploy form: enable Pocket ID
        sign-in, the issuer URL, the client ID, the client secret, and
        the provider display name if you want a different label. The
        issuer URL is your Pocket ID instance's public URL with no
        trailing slash. It must match exactly, because Vikunja fetches
        its discovery document from that address.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Vikunja sends no PKCE challenge, so the client you created with
        PKCE off is the matching shape. The callback path stays
        /auth/openid/pocketid whatever the display name says, because
        the path comes from the settings the platform fills, not the
        label. Username and password sign-in keeps working alongside
        Pocket ID.
      </p>
    </.card>

    <.card class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-base font-medium text-ink">If Pocket ID goes down or away</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Connected apps degrade. They do not die. Already-signed-in
        sessions persist. New sign-ins fail while the issuer is
        unreachable and come back when it is reachable again.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted" data-testid="destroy-loses-identities">
        Destroying the Pocket ID instance is the permanent case. A
        destroy deletes the instance and its data for good. The
        identities it holds are lost with it, unless you can restore
        from a backup you own. A restore needs the backup password and
        the encryption key you chose at deploy, so save both somewhere
        safe today. Restoring into a new instance is manual work today.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Keep one local way in per app. Forgejo and Memos keep their
        local logins. Kavita's recovery is documented upstream. Remove
        the OIDC block and password login returns. Vaultwarden always
        keeps the master password. ActualBudget keeps the server
        password unless you enforced OpenID. Vikunja keeps username and
        password sign-in. The platform's own sign-in never depends on
        Pocket ID.
      </p>
    </.card>
  </div>
</Layouts.public>
